  "text": "**Solution 1 (f(q) = 2q − 3) is the most likely correct answer, but I could not verify it.** I'm recalling the log Severi-type inequality for surfaces without maximal Albanese dimension from memory, not from a checked source.\n\n**Solution 3 (q − 1) is wrong.** Its key step, \"κ̄ ≥ 0 implies χ̄ ≥ 0, so P̄₁ ≥ q − 1,\" is asserted without justification and does not follow from the general theory. Its sharpness example is also weak: a hyperelliptic surface has q = 1, so it only checks the bound at one point. Its \"general\" case analysis is hand-waved, and it never exhibits a family attaining the bound for q ≥ 2.\n\n**Solution 2 (2q − 2) is unlikely.** Nothing I can reconstruct points to 2q − 2 rather than 2q − 3, and the sharpness claim is unsupported.\n\n**Solution 1 (2q − 3)** matches the pattern of known Castelnuovo–de Franchis-type bounds: the general-type inequality for maximal Albanese dimension is p_g ≥ 2q − 4, and the non-maximal case is typically one step weaker. Its justification is also thin, since it appeals to a \"Castelnuovo–Beauville-type\" analogy rather than a derivation, but it is the only candidate consistent with that pattern.\n\nCHOICE: 1",
